Implementing a multi-tree list within an Angular application involves creating multiple tree structures and allowing them to be navigated and interacted with together. Below is a high-level guide on how you can achieve this:

### 1. Set Up Your Project

First, ensure you have Node.js installed. Then create a new Angular project using the following command:

```bash
ng new multiTreeListApp
cd multiTreeListApp
```

### 2. Create the Tree Structures

You'll need two types of tree lists: one for each type of data (e.g., categories and subcategories).

#### Category Tree

Create a component for the category tree.

```bash
ng generate component category-list
```

In `category-list.component.ts`:
```typescript
import { Component } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-category-list',
  templateUrl: './category-list.component.html',
  styleUrls: ['./category-list.component.css']
})
export class CategoryListComponent {
  categories = [
    { id: 1, name: 'Electronics', children: [] },
    { id: 2, name: 'Books', children: [] },
    // Add more categories as needed
  ];

  selectedCategory?: any;

  onSelect(category: any) {
    this.selectedCategory = category;
  }
}
```

In `category-list.component.html`:
```html
<ul>
  <li *ngFor="let category of categories" (click)="onSelect(category)">
    {{ category.name }}
    <ul *ngIf="category.children">
      <li *ngFor="let child of category.children">
        {{ child.name }}
      </li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ul>
```

#### Subcategory Tree

Similarly, create another component for the subcategories.

```bash
ng generate component subcategory-list
```

In `subcategory-list.component.ts`:
